posts.body:
@TheAM-Dol
i never said anything about bad games. don't be sticking words in my mouth.
ive played bad games, and rarely are they jrpgs. in fact, i can;t think of many. possibly bards tale on nes, because the final boss cheats worse than chaos in final fantasy with full heals. i point toward final fantasy x when i say bad, as far as combat goes, because swapping characters out to be able to damage certain monsters is horrendous. neat story though.
it's a variety of 'encounter off' in games, which has always irked me to no end. encounters are there to prepare you, gain you free resources, drain said resources along the way. to make you decide to risk a percent chance to escape and lose more resources, or succeed, making the game play more random. i never said COMBAT was a free ride, progress in the game nearly is, since you can specifically plan for six (insert actual number) encounters to be properly leveled, and hoard everything you have, to make end game easier and easier. it's much harder to balance against overwhelming currency collection, which can kind of make all of it moot, depending on the variety of items you have.
@Sword_of_Dusk
you're conflating trying to attack bosses without enemy encounters, to what i actually said, which was implied in the 'pick and choose' portion of that. also, i point you at speed runs of final fantasy 8 for under-leveling in a JRPG.

@ZimXero
no, not 'the two games' just 'two games,' and those are bad in my opinion, and it's only parts of them. which vaguely have a point in this thread. slow mid battle party swaps to be able to attack weaknesses is tedious beyond my ability to play, i never beat X, because that part made me want to frisbee the disk down the street. had to read the plot online. which might have been fine during bosses, but during 1/3++ of encounters? eww. bards tale is an edge case in this, that it's not about randoms, final bosses just shouldn't be allowed wanton access to the strongest in battle heal spell in the game (strong enough to wipe out all your progress and then some) with no restrictions on casting multiple times in a row.
@Sword_of_Dusk
still pick and choose, because when you get the level you desire, you essentially flick on 'encounters off' and skip on over to the boss. and the speed run is for the 9 hour version of ff8, it's not needed to be a pro to do it at low level. more of a platformer that way: kill the things in your way, dodge the rest, swing on boss until it's HP forgets to stay above 1.
@Canned_Dinosaur
i'm not precisely crapping on it for the sake of crapping on it, i'm distributing a valid opinion on the topic, and someone got all snarky with the part of the opinion that chrono trigger and secret of mana were easy mode (which i still stand by). that apparently raised people's ire. and someone being negative hasn't hurt my feelings since k-5th. every point of view can open up potentiality toward bits one didn't know before, and maybe someone else will benefit from it down the line, let's no go around getting people to stop talking, because someone disagrees with someone else.
@Tai_MT
argument is the paragraph above this one. it's odd too, i never even mentioned ff mystic quest as bad, and it has precisely the encounters that are the kind i dislike xD my opinion was, on screen encounters make games simpler, and it takes away from the original design idea behind the genre. and then someone started saying i called their game bad, and blah blah, usual chat room stuff. OTHERWISE, you bring up the best point is about how fun a game should be, and interestingly enough, a 65 person poll will never be a large enough sample size to decide going off the norm is or is not the right move fiscally. and personal taste is rarely a reason to make fiscal choices either. probably why non of us work for square-enix.
